  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure centered over the lower Great Lakes early this \nmorning, will track east into NY State today. This will provide \ncontinue cool mornings and seasonably warm daytime temperatures \ntoday through Tuesday. Patchy fog will likely occur in typical \nfavored river/sheltered valleys into early this morning and \nagain tonight. A short-wave trough aloft and weak cold front \nwill move across New England on Tue bringing some extra clouds, \nhowever high pressure will become re-established by late Tue.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter a multi-day stretch of dry weather, unsettled conditions \nare expected Wed into early Thu as a progressive upper level \ntrough and associated frontal system move across the region. \nInitially on Wed, showers will be mainly confined to areas \nnorth/west of Albany associated with the system's warm front, \nwith showers spreading south/east across the rest of the area \nWed night into early Thu as the system's cold front sweeps \nthrough the area. Will only mention slight chance of thunder due\nto limited instability. Deep layer moisture does increase, with\nPWAT anomalies forecast to rise to +1 to +2 STDEV, so most \nareas should see at least some rainfall although flooding is not\nanticipated due to lack of convection and progressive movement \nof the system. It will turn humid before a drier air mass starts\nto build back in behind the cold front on Thu. It will also \nturn breezy during this time, first with southerly winds on Wed,\nthen westerly winds behind the cold front on Thu with \noccasional gusts of 20-30 mph.",
